//? Title: Index Calculus: Actual Relations, Composite Orders and Reusable Logarithms
//? Subtitle: A checked uint64 finite-field study with explicit bounded failures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a prime p, a primitive generator g and a nonzero target h, find the
exponent x with g raised to x congruent to h modulo p. Index calculus converts
smooth powers into equations for a small set of prime logarithms, then reuses
that table for target queries. We start modulo five, inspect actual relations
and prime-power lifts, preserve the original four cases and show a genuinely
rank-deficient batch. The original asymptotic curves are retained as
illustrative formulas, not measurements of this bounded implementation or
cryptographic security estimates.
)
\toc

> A complete example modulo five

Let p=5, g=2 and h=4. The multiplicative group has order four, and the valid
factor base is {2,3}; primes at least five are excluded because they do not
represent the required positive integer factors below p. The logarithms are
log₂2=1 and log₂3=3 modulo four. A pre-change probe of the old implementation
returned failure for this valid case because its base included unusable primes.

The program prints the first four accepted relations, explicitly a selected
subset of the complete history. All actual linear stages and verified base logs
are printed. The initial solve modulo two gives only the parity of each log; the
lift to modulus four recovers their full values. Finally the actual target shift
and smooth factorization produce x=2, checked by exponentiation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/index_calculus_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/index_calculus_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/modular_exponentiation.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::index_calculus_prepare_trace(2, 5);
    std::print("First four of {} actual accepted relations.\n",
               t.relations.size());
    std::print("| actual attempt | exponent | residue | powers of [2, 3] |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i =
         0; i < std::min(std::size_t(4), t.relations.size()); ++i) {
        const auto &r = t.relations[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.attempt, r.exponent, r.residue,
                   r.powers);
    }
    std::print("| prime field | resulting modulus | actual rank | actual solution |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "--- |\n");
    for (const auto &stage : t.stages)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", stage.prime, stage.modulus,
                   stage.rank, stage.solution);
    const auto q = dm::algorithms::index_calculus_query(t.table, 4);
    std::print("Verified base logs {}. Target 4: actual shift {}, residue {}, powers {}, returned x {}, " "exponent check {}.\n",
               t.table.logarithms, q.shift, q.residue, q.powers, q.result.x,
               dm::algorithms::mod_pow(2, q.result.x, 5) == 4);
    dm::algorithms::index_calculus_preparation_diagram(t).save_svg(
        "build/index_calculus_tiny.svg");
}
```
