> Why smooth powers give linear equations

For a primitive generator g of the prime field's nonzero group, every nonzero
residue has a unique logarithm modulo n=p−1. Choose primes qⱼ≤B with qⱼ<p. If an
actual residue has the ordinary integer factorization
\(g^a\bmod p=\prod_jq_j^{e_j}\), then

$$
\sum_j e_j L_j\equiv a\pmod n,\qquad L_j=\log_gq_j.
$$
\alttext(Each accepted smooth power yields one modular linear equation for the factor-base logarithms.)

Residues are factored as positive integers, not by a finite-field polynomial
factorization. Rejected nonsmooth trials increase the attempt/test counters but
are not retained as relation rows. The sampler preserves the original xorshift
recurrence and samples a in 1 through p−2, excluding the trivial identity power;
this differs from textbook sampling that also permits exponent zero.

> The group order is a ring modulus, not a prime field

Even for prime p, n=p−1 is generally composite. For example, two has no inverse
modulo four: the equation 2L=2 has solutions L=1 and L=3, and dividing by two as
if modulo a field would discard information. Merely finding some invertible
pivots modulo n can also miss systems that are solvable using different pivots
modulo its distinct prime factors.

The kernel factors n into prime powers. It solves A·L=b modulo each prime q
using genuine field pivots and requires full column rank there. Given a solution
x modulo qʲ, write its lift as x+qʲy. Substitution gives a new field system:

$$
A y\equiv\frac{b-Ax}{q^j}\pmod q.
$$
\alttext(A correction y lifts a solution from modulus q to the j to modulus q to the j plus one by solving the divided residual modulo q.)

The residual is computed modulo qʲ⁺¹; its representative must be divisible by
qʲ, making the displayed quotient well-defined modulo q. Repeating produces a
solution for each full prime power. For mutually coprime completed moduli M,m,
combine an assembled value x and new residue y with

$$
x_{new}=x+M\bigl((y-x)M^{-1}\bmod m\bigr)\pmod{Mm}.
$$
\alttext(The Chinese remainder update matches the old residue modulo M and the new residue modulo m.)

Exact widened products and overflow-safe reduced addition/subtraction support
the full uint64 modulus domain. Every completed base log is checked by powering
g back to its prime. Incomplete rank or exhausted relation work returns an
explicit unready status, never a fabricated solution. The generator certificate
requires factoring p−1; its extra work is not hidden in a claim about
index-calculus speed. This is a dense educational implementation, with no
generic-log fallback.

For R accepted rows and K base primes, a dense field solve costs O(R·K²) modular
operations. There is one such solve per prime-power level; lifting residuals add
O(R·K) operations per extra level. Ordinary storage is O(B+R·K), including
working matrix copies; tracing adds O((R+stages)·K) retained values. A
smoothness test uses K final nondivisibility tests plus at most log₂(p)
successful divisions, and each trial also powers modulo p. These operation
counts exclude the separate group certificate and public-query table checks.
They do not turn a fixed capped implementation into a proven subexponential
bit-complexity algorithm.

> Reuse the table: the original four cases

For a target h, search an actual shift s for which
\(h g^s\bmod p=\prod_jq_j^{f_j}\). The already verified base logs then give

$$
x\equiv\sum_j f_jL_j-s\pmod n.
$$
\alttext(The target logarithm is the factor-exponent dot product with the stored logs, minus the actual sampled shift.)

The returned exponent is checked against h. Public queries validate the complete
borrowed table, group-factor certificate and supplied base logs before target
search; this validation has a cost beyond the counted attempts. Each query
starts a copy of the preparation's final RNG state, so the table is immutable
across queries and repeated calls are reproducible. Preparation is genuinely
reused: no relation matrix is recollected for a new target. The legacy one-shot
wrapper still prepares afresh for each nontrivial target.

The next block preserves all four original p,g,x cases and their exact targets.
Its two panels count actual relation and target trials. Four successful examples
do not establish a timing law, a success probability or a runtime crossover.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/index_calculus_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/index_calculus_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/modular_exponentiation.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    struct Case {
        std::uint64_t p, g, x;
    };
    const std::vector<Case> cases{{10007, 5, 1234}, {100003, 2, 54321},
                                  {1000003, 2, 424242}, {15485863, 6, 9999999}};
    std::vector<double> bits, preparation, queries;
    std::print("| p | g | actual target | hidden exponent | recovered exponent | verified | relation trials " "| target trials |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto c : cases) {
        const auto h = dm::algorithms::mod_pow(c.g, c.x, c.p);
        const auto t = dm::algorithms::index_calculus_prepare(c.g, c.p);
        const auto q = dm::algorithms::index_calculus_query(t, h);
        if (!q.result.found)
            throw std::runtime_error("original case failed");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", c.p, c.g, h,
                   c.x, q.result.x,
                   dm::algorithms::mod_pow(c.g, q.result.x, c.p) == h,
                   t.work.relation_attempts, q.attempts);
        bits.push_back(std::log2(double(c.p)));
        preparation.push_back(t.work.relation_attempts);
        queries.push_back(q.attempts);
    }
    auto a = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    a.line(bits, preparation, "relations", {37, 99, 235}, 2).title(
        "Actual relation trials").x_label("log2(p)").y_label("trials");
    auto b = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    b.line(bits, queries, "queries", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title(
        "Actual target trials").x_label("log2(p)").y_label("trials");
    auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
    layout.add(a).add(b).size(1100, 500).title("Four original cases: selected work, not runtime").fit_panels().save_svg("build/index_calculus_original.svg");
}
```
