> Validation and reference

Sanitizer tests cover every target for primes through 199 with a larger relation
batch, verifying actual accepted products and every recorded prime-power system.
They include the four original cases, power-of-two lifts, honest rank failure,
complete caps, zero budgets, ownership, invalid inputs and corrupted public
tables. A full-width prime control checks a supplied verified one-prime table
and a forced smooth shift; it does not claim that random precomputation solved a
cryptographic- sized problem. New graphics and model controls check complete
display admission, failed-stage diagrams, invalid model inputs and stable
log-space evaluation. An independent Python integer oracle checks 486
preparations, 17997 actual accepted relations, 1273 prime-field/lift stages and
2430 queries, including the RNG stream, statuses and all selected counters. Of
these preparations, 331 are ready, 146 rank deficient and nine relation limited.
Frozen comparisons preserve all 211 old successes among 256 seeded
original-prime cases; six of the old 45 failures now solve. Fresh coverage
checks pass 90 definitions, 16 compiled public examples and four representative
clangd hovers.

The technical reference is [Menezes, van Oorschot and Vanstone, Handbook of
Applied Cryptography, Chapter 3](https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/hac/about/chap3.pdf),
Algorithm 3.68 and Example 3.69, with Notes 3.71 and 3.73 on parameter choice
and reused precomputation. This report's exact prime-power linear algebra, fixed
bounded batch and sampler convention are explicit implementation choices. A
small verified demonstration is evidence of arithmetic correctness on those
cases, not a practical cryptanalytic performance claim.
